\subsection{Start playback from the tag in batch mode}

This step realises the tutorial's central contrast: the same batch
playback as the first step, with only the starting point varied.
Its object of learning is that a tag changes where playback begins.
The expert extra resolves an apparent mismatch the novice never sees:
the tag names one commit while a run is stored as a pair, and playback
resolves the tag to the run record it belongs to.

<<shared step: start playback from the tag in batch mode>>=
# Start playback from the tag in batch mode

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: 'learnlog\s+play\b(?:[^\n]*(?:-b\b|--batch\b)[^\n]*\s+(?!-)\S+|\s+(?!-)\S+[^\n]*(?:-b\b|--batch\b))'
hint: Pass the tag name after `learnlog play -b`.
```

Use `learnlog play -b lab1` to start playback from the tagged commit
instead of from the beginning of the history.
<<novice step: start playback from the tag in batch mode>>=
<<shared step: start playback from the tag in batch mode>>
<<expert step: start playback from the tag in batch mode>>=
<<shared step: start playback from the tag in batch mode>>

The tag names one commit, but a run is stored as a pair of commits;
playback resolves the tag to the run record it belongs to and starts
there.
@

\subsection{Open the interactive viewer from the tag}

The closing step varies the medium while the anchor stays invariant:
the same tag now opens the interactive viewer.
The prose diverges only in register---the novice variant avoids the
word \emph{curses}---and the expert extra completes the
reviewing-is-recorded thread from the first step: the viewer reports
what the learner looks at, and a keypress that does not change the
displayed commit records nothing.

<<shared step: open the interactive viewer from the tag>>=
# Open the interactive viewer from the tag

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: 'learnlog\s+play\s+(?!-)\S+'
hint: Launch the interactive viewer and quit with `q` when you are done.
```
<<novice step: open the interactive viewer from the tag>>=
<<shared step: open the interactive viewer from the tag>>

Run `learnlog play lab1` to open the interactive viewer at the tagged
commit.
Move around a little, then quit with `q`.
<<expert step: open the interactive viewer from the tag>>=
<<shared step: open the interactive viewer from the tag>>

Run `learnlog play lab1` to open the curses viewer at the tagged commit.
Move around a little, then quit with `q`.

The viewer also reports what you look at: opening a commit and
stepping or jumping between commits go into the review trace, while a
keypress that does not change the displayed commit records nothing.
@

\subsection{Measure one milestone}

The added step closes the loop the tag opened: the name the learner
chose is now an argument a report accepts, which is the payoff that
makes tagging worth the habit.
It sits here rather than in \cref{tut:metrics} to stay
next to the tag it reads; the grouped series belongs to the metrics
tutorial.

<<shared step: measure one milestone>>=
# Measure one milestone

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: 'learnlog\s+metrics\s+\w+[^\n]*--since\s+\S+'
hint: Try `learnlog metrics time --since lab1`.
```

Your tag is also a boundary for the metrics: run
`learnlog metrics time --since lab1` to summarise only the work from
that starting point on.
<<novice step: measure one milestone>>=
<<shared step: measure one milestone>>
<<expert step: measure one milestone>>=
<<shared step: measure one milestone>>

`--since lab1` starts the window at the tagged run, inclusive, and
`--until` another tag would end it just before that tag's run: the
half-open window is exactly what start marker means.
@

\subsection{Go back to the tagged version}

The closing pair turns the tag from something the learner reads into
something they act on.
This step's object of learning is that an earlier version is not
merely viewable but recoverable, and that recovering it is safe.
The learner first makes a change and runs it, so that there is
something to lose, and only then asks for the tagged version back:
without that preparation the checkout would change nothing visible
and the step would teach a command without its consequence.
The tag is the anchor of the earlier steps, kept invariant so that the
only thing that varies is the operation---[[play lab1]] showed the
version, [[checkout lab1]] restores it.
The instruction sends the learner to the file and to [[learnlog list]]
afterwards, because the two observations together carry the point:
the change is gone from the file but not from the log, which lists
both the run that made it and the checkout that replaced it.
The expert extra names the mechanism that makes this safe: the
checkout records the files it is about to replace before it touches
them, and the restored files become the tip of the same branch, so
[[HEAD]] is never left detached the way a raw [[git checkout]] would
leave it.

<<shared step: go back to the tagged version>>=
# Go back to the tagged version

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: 'learnlog\s+checkout\s+(?!-)\S+'
  - next run continues
hint: Run `learnlog checkout lab1` to get the tagged files back.
```

Change something in your program and run it once more.
Then run `learnlog checkout lab1` to get the tagged version of your
files back.
Open the file: your change is gone.
Run `learnlog list`: the log shows both the run that made the change
and the checkout that replaced it.
<<novice step: go back to the tagged version>>=
<<shared step: go back to the tagged version>>
<<expert step: go back to the tagged version>>=
<<shared step: go back to the tagged version>>

The checkout records itself before it touches a file: the edit you
just discarded is a commit in the hidden repository, so nothing is
lost.
The restored files then become the tip of the same branch, and `HEAD`
is never left detached; your next run simply continues the log from
the restored version.
@

\subsection{Come forward again}

The final step varies only the direction.
The command is the same, the argument is now the hash the previous
step printed on its \enquote{recorded as} line, and the discarded
files come back.
That is the tutorial's last contrast: a checkout is undone by another
checkout, because the log never rewrites anything, and both states
stay in it.
The step also returns the learner's project to the state it was in
before the previous step, so the tutorial leaves no surprise behind.
Requiring a hash rather than a name is what distinguishes this step
from the previous one in the transcript, and it is also the habit the
step teaches: the confirmation line names the way back, and the way
back is a run's hash.

<<shared step: come forward again>>=
# Come forward again

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: 'learnlog\s+checkout\s+[0-9a-f]{7,40}\b'
  - next run continues
hint: Pass the hash from the `recorded as` line to `learnlog checkout`.
```

The previous step printed where your discarded files were recorded,
on the line starting `Your previous files were recorded as`.
Check out that hash to get them back.
Your project is now as it was before you went back, and the log holds
both versions.
<<novice step: come forward again>>=
<<shared step: come forward again>>
<<expert step: come forward again>>=
<<shared step: come forward again>>

A checkout is undone by another checkout because nothing is ever
rewritten: the snapshot the first checkout made is an ordinary run
record on the branch, and checking it out records the restored
version in turn.
@
